==== Apadana Palace coin hoard

Apadana hoard

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/KINGS_of_LYDIA._Time_of_Cyrus_to_Darios_I._Circa_545-520_BC.jpg/250px-KINGS_of_LYDIA._Time_of_Cyrus_to_Darios_I._Circa_545-520_BC.jpg" height="67" width="164"

Gold [[ Croeseid ]] minted in the time of [[ Darius | darius-i ]], of the type
of the eight Croeseids found in the Apadana hoard, c.545–520 BC. Light series:
8.07g (0.285oz), [[ Sardis ]] mint.

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Aegina_Stater_achaic.jpg/250px-Aegina_Stater_achaic.jpg" height="68" width="128"

Type of the [[ Aegina ]] stater found in the Apadana hoard, 550–530BC.
Obverse: [[ Sea turtle ]] with large pellets down centre. Reverse: incuse
square punch with eight sections.[[ ^48 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/THRACE%2C_Abdera._Circa_540-35-520-15_BC.jpg/250px-THRACE%2C_Abdera._Circa_540-35-520-15_BC.jpg" height="68" width="150"

Type of the [[ Abdera | abdera-thrace ]] coin found in the Apadana hoard,
c.540/35–520/15 BC. Obverse: Griffin seated left, raising paw. Reverse:
Quadripartite incuse square.[[ ^48 ]]

The [[ Apadana hoard ]] is a hoard of coins that were discovered under the
stone boxes containing the foundation tablets of the [[ Apadana Palace ]] in
Persepolis.[[ ^48 ]] The coins were discovered in excavations in 1933 by
[[ Erich Schmidt | erich-schmidt-archaeologist ]], in two deposits, each
deposit under the two deposition boxes that were found. The deposition of this
hoard is dated to c. 515 BC.[[ ^48 ]] The coins consisted in eight gold
lightweight [[ Croeseids | croeseid ]], a [[ tetradrachm ]] of
[[ Abdera | abdera-thrace ]], a stater of [[ Aegina ]] and three
double-[[ sigloi ]] from [[ Cyprus ]].[[ ^48 ]] The Croeseids were found in
very fresh condition, confirming that they had been recently minted under
Achaemenid rule.[[ ^52 ]] The deposit did not have any [[ Darics | daric ]]
and [[ Sigloi ]], which also suggests strongly that these coins typical of
[[ Achaemenid coinage ]] only started to be minted later, after the foundation
of the Apadana Palace.[[ ^52 ]]

=== The Throne Hall

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg/250px-Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg" height="166" width="250"

  <figcaption>The Throne Hall, Persepolis

Next to the Apadana, second largest building of the Terrace and the final
edifices, is the Throne Hall or the Imperial Army's //Hall of Honor// (also
called the //Hundred-Columns Palace//). This 70 m^^2^^ × 70 m^^2^^ (750 sq ft
× 750 sq ft) hall was started by [[ Xerxes I ]] and completed by his son
[[ Artaxerxes I | artaxerxes-i-of-persia ]] by the end of the fifth century
BC. Its eight stone doorways are decorated on the south and north with reliefs
of throne scenes and on the east and west with scenes depicting the king in
combat with monsters. Two colossal stone bulls flank the northern portico. The
head of one of the bulls now resides in the
[[ Oriental Institute | university-of-chicago-oriental-institute ]] in
Chicago[[ ^53 ]] and a column base from one of the columns in the
[[ British Museum ]].[[ ^54 ]]

At the beginning of the reign of Xerxes I, the Throne Hall was used mainly for
receptions for military commanders and representatives of all the subject
nations of the empire. Later, the Throne Hall served as an imperial museum.

=== The Tachara Palace

[[ Tachara ]], was the exclusive palace of [[ Darius the Great ]] at
Persepolis. Only a small portion of the palace was finished under his rule, it
was completed after the death of Darius in 486 BC, by his son and successor,
[[ Xerxes | xerxes-i ]],[[ ^55 ]] who called it a Taçara, which means "winter
palace" in Old Persian. It was then used by [[ Artaxerxes I ]]. In the 4th
century BC, following his invasion of Iran in 330 BC,
[[ Alexander the Great ]] allowed his troops to loot Persepolis. This palace
was one of the few structures that escaped destruction in the burning of the
complex by Alexander's army, and because of that, Tachara is the most intact
building of Persepolis today. It is also the oldest structure at Persepolis.
Tachara stands back to back to the [[ Apadana ]], and is oriented
southward.[[ ^56 ]]

